        <body>
            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0362" n="334" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v23p1/pg334"/>

                <head>163. Memorandum of a Conversation Between the Secretary of State and Senator
                    William F. Knowland, Washington, May 28, 1957<anchor n="1" xml:id="d163-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                ><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1957-05-28">May 28,
                                1957</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">MEMORANDUM OF CONVERSATION WITH SENATOR KNOWLAND AT THE
                    GERMAN EMBASSY</p>
                <p>I said to Knowland that we had concluded that there was no practical alternative
                    to allowing the Japanese to go ahead and exercise jurisdiction in the Girard
                    case. I pointed out that this had already been agreed to and I saw no practical
                    way of retracting our steps without throwing doubt upon the value of our
                    agreements and raising a storm which might sweep us out of all the Western
                    Pacific. Knowland said that would be “tough” but he did not indicate either
                    approval or disapproval.</p>
                <p>I talked in a like manner to Senator Alex Smith<anchor n="2" xml:id="d163-f2"/>
                    and to Congressmen GORDON<anchor n="3" xml:id="d163-f3"/> and
                        Chiperfield.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d163-f4"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">JFD</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
